#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 13pt, weight: "bold")[Lang Tips\/Accent]]
Accents are tricky and a hard thing to get down. Unfortunately there is no way to completely get rid of your accent. You can make it better and keep working at it, but truly perfecting the accent would take years. \(Example: German speakers speaking english. It is perfect and the accent is great, but there are still words from time to time where an accent is present. And they study english for years.\) Focusing on the accent, or working on it, is completely up to each and every missionary. Some prioritize it more than others. I prioritized it because I didnt want to sound like I was taking a high-school level spanish class. \(Hhhhhhola amigo. #emph[#strong[imagine the most white accent ever haha]]\) Anyways, Here are some of my notes from my mission. It\'s not perfect and by all means, make a copy of this and make correcrions as needed, but these are the things I have noticed.

#v(0.3em) #line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.4pt + luma(200)) #v(0.3em)
#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[General Accent Rules]]
These are comments or rules that specifically apply to accentation of words and speech.

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
- Accent changes depending on the case sometimes. \(Especially in genative with the reappearing a. Watch out\) Not super hard, just takes practice
- Accent will also change sometimes depending on whether or not the verb is perfective or Imperfective. \(This relates more to the length. It varies\)
- Additionally, the accent changes on some verbs depending on the tense it is in \(past, present, future\)
- In Serbian, stress usually falls on the first syllable of the word. If the word is longer \(usually 3 or more syllables, then the stress will be on the second to last or the third to last syllable. THIS RULE ISNT TRUE FOR ALL WORDS. THIS IS JUST A GUIDELINE. WATCH OUT!\)
- Diminutives \(smaller versions of things\): When adding suffixes to a word, the stress often remains on the same syllable. \(Example: kuća → kućica\)
- Compound Words: Each component retains its original stress. \(Example: čokolada + kolač = čokoladakolač\)
- Loanwords: Stress placement may follow the original language\'s rules or adapt to Serbian patterns over time \(Example: kompjúter, šokoláda\)
- In Serbian, stressed syllables often contain long vowels, while unstressed syllables usually have short vowels. \(People from Čačak and southern Serbia \"over stress\" their words and they drag out their vowels. They make the stress longer. This causes them to talk slower and it takes more time to talk because of it. It\'s the reason some people see the accent\/people from Čačak\/southern Serbia as stupid\)
- The stress often remains on the stem of a word. Certain endings, such as the genitive plural \"-a\", can affect stress placement \(like i mentioned above\) \(Example: knjìga → knjígama. The accent goes from a long to a short\)
- In Serbian, the accent placement in verbs changes when transitioning from the present tense to the imperative form \(we call this command form\). In the present tense, the stress is usually on the root or the suffix of the verb \(aka the normal normal spot\). However, in the imperative form, the stress shifts to the ending of the verb. For example, in the present tense verb \"razmišljam\" \(I am thinking\), the stress is on the root \"miš,\" whereas in the imperative \"razmišljaj\" \(Think!\), the stress shifts to the ending \"-aj.\" This accent change is a key distinction between these two verb forms
]
#v(0.3em) #line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.4pt + luma(200)) #v(0.3em)
#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[General Language-Learning Tips]]
Here are some general tips for this beautiful language. These can are totally random. Uživaj!

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
- Never accent on last syllable, if you cannot tell which syllable has the marking, guess it is the second to last one.
- If a second to last syllable is an i the third to last syllable probably has the stress.
- If a word ends in -cija, the syllable just before probably has the stress.
- Feel it out. Test with different words. While reading the BoM just try and focus on pronunciation every once in a while. \(ex: noć igara, akcija, putovanje, zavarivanje\) difference between native and Serbish
- I\'m a very hands on kinda person. So match verbs or words with actions. Sometimes I find myself looking up how to say stuff, but I find that I learn better when I match a word or something either an action or phrase. Sometimes I draw it in ny notebook instead of writing it down in english. Sometimes I act it out. just depends. kinda like a kids picture book. I know Elder Larson does that a lot.
- like \"heklanje\" for example. I went to this old members house in Osijek and she talked for ever and ever about Heklanje and I had absolutely no idea what it was. so I asked her and she held up a needle and some yarn. Now when I see that all I think is heklanje. I don\'t think \"oh that\'s crocheting\" \"what is that in language....\" so maybe you have a little vocab picture book or something. idk just and idea. It skips that extra step in your head. \(Some missionaries call it translating in your head - Going from what you see, to english, to serbian, then saying it\)
- Listening to and reading something at the same time. this can be like a conference talk or a Harry Potter book or even a YouTube video with subtitles. but following along and listening to how things are said I feel like just help language skills improve in general. especially with pronunciation and stress on words.
- Shadowing: Listen to audio recordings or native speakers and repeat what they say immediately after them. This exercise helps improve your pronunciation, fluency, and speed.
- Tongue twisters: Practice tongue twisters in the foreign language to develop agility and coordination of speech muscles. Start slowly and gradually increase your speed.
- Rapid-fire conversations: Engage in conversations with native speakers or language partners where you focus on responding quickly. Try to minimize pauses and push yourself to think and respond faster.
- Reading aloud: Choose texts in the foreign language and read them aloud at an accelerated pace. Increase your speed gradually, aiming for smooth delivery without sacrificing comprehension.
- Language games: Play vocabulary-based games like word association or rapid-fire question-and-answer sessions with a language partner or group. These activities enhance your mental agility and encourage quick thinking.
- Immersion: Immerse yourself in the language by watching movies, TV shows, or listening to podcasts in the foreign language. Exposure to natural speech patterns and fast-paced conversations will help you adapt to the language\'s rhythm and cadence. \(I realise that this one doesnt really apply anymore with our new mission president, so maybe use this one when you get home.\)
- My Daily Words\/Language questions i write down on a Google note and set a reminder on them \(all possible in the app\) in order to help me remember and review the words I hear throughout the day. Super quick and easy. Better than taking a notepad around all day
- flashcards \(dont just make them the word\) \(funny phrase, specific rule\/case difference\)
- Everytime I learned a new word, I would add it to my dictionary. I just kinda made it for fun to gauge my learning and my progress, but its a fun idea if you want. You can color code it and do all kinds of fun stuff with it.
- Frequency Words. when you miss sections of the conversation, find the most high-frequency and important words for the topic and memorize them
- Teach grammar principles and words you learn to your companion, even if you learned it from them
- study and pray about chapter 7 of Preach My Gospel
- Listen to a song a few times, read and translate the transcript, read along with the song, then just listen and notice increased comprehension
- pray consistently for gift of tongues and interpretation of tongues \(you know what they\'re saying without knowing the words, understanding is given through the spirit\)
- Learn the vocabulary to tell a story
- 3-day learning period on vocab \(drill words during study, then actively review them for the next 3 days while continuing to learn new words and encorporate them throughout the day\)
- Actively recall words you\'ve learned while walking
- if you\'re not talking, have a conversation about something in your mind, focus either on getting grammar entirely correct or maintaining fluid Croatian and ignore grammar entirely
- make a list of words to learn, high frequency is best, and keep track of how much you\'re learning
- Read book of mormon out-loud in language, don\'t worry about comprehension \(select high-frequency words through the chapters, then memorize them and actively recall the translation while you read\)
- Speak to natives as much as possible
- Write notes, reminders, Area Book, etc. in language \(but not in your journal because you need time to relax and write freely\)
- diligently SYL or full-English fast, especially pray in language \(don\'t substitute English words in, find a different way to say it and mark down the word to memorize later\)
- when people speak to you in English, speak back in Croatian, especially members at church and with other missionaries who speak Croatian
- read a story in the Friend or children Book of Mormon stories to practice comprehension \(easier and less intimidating than Book of Mormon\)
- listen to a conversation between your companion and a native with the intent to summarize everything that was said, then summarize to the best of your ability and have your companion fill in what you missed
- Ask other missionaries for support, tell them about your goals, and ask them questions
- Hrvatska Jezična Portal is a great way to look up words in croatian \(Idk if they have one for Serbian\)
- If you want to overcome your nervousness and improve your speaking ability in Serbian, here are some things to practice for 10-15 minutes each:
  #block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
    - Record Yourself Speaking: Practice speaking Serbian by recording yourself speaking about a chosen topic or simply reading out loud. Listen to your recordings to identify areas for improvement.
    - Speaking with a Language Partner: Find a language partner or tutor who speaks Serbian fluently and practice conversing with them regularly. This will help you gain confidence in real-life conversations. This can be other missionaries at your same level or better. Of course it is always better when you practice with someone who is better than you.
    - Positive Affirmations and Visualization: Before speaking in Serbian, practice positive affirmations and visualize successful interactions.
    - Role-playing: This is actually the worst, but it is effective. Ugh
    - Storytelling: Tell short stories or anecdotes in Serbian, focusing on using a variety of vocabulary and proper grammar. This exercise helps improve fluency and creativity in speaking.
    - Language Games: Play language games like that \"20 Questions\" in Serbian with your comp. This makes practicing speaking more enjoyable and helps expand vocabulary.
    - Translate short texts or articles from your native language into the target language, and vice versa.
      #block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
          - Analyze translations by comparing your version with professional translations.
          - Practice paraphrasing sentences and expressions to convey the same meaning in different ways.
          - Seek feedback from native speakers or language instructors on your translations
      ]
  ]
]
