#emph[The following pronunciation section was not in Elder Mackay\'s original notes — added here to extend his accent material with concrete tongue-placement guidance.]

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 13pt, weight: "bold")[Pronunciation: The Practical Stuff]]
#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[č\/ć and dž\/đ]]
This is the single hardest thing for English speakers to get right, ngl. English kind of has both sounds but you\'ve never had to consciously tell them apart, so your mouth doesn\'t know what to do.

#strong[č] is like the \"ch\" in \"church,\" but harder and further back in your mouth#degmark  — the tip of your tongue curls up toward the back of the ridge behind your teeth, almost toward the roof of your mouth#degmark . It\'s a heavier, thicker sound.

#strong[ć] is softer and further forward#degmark , closer to the \"t\" in \"future\" or \"actual\" said fast#degmark  — the front of your tongue is right up against the alveolar ridge \(the bumpy part right behind your top teeth\)#degmark , not curled back at all. It\'s a lighter, thinner sound than č.

Same relationship applies to the voiced pair:

#strong[dž] is the voiced version of č#degmark  \(same tongue position, but you buzz your vocal cords\) — think the \"j\" in \"jeep\" or \"judge,\" but heavier#degmark .

#strong[đ] is the voiced version of ć#degmark  — softer, more forward, closer to the \"j\"\/\"d\" sound you\'d hear in \"soldier\" or \"adjust\" said quickly#degmark , not the full hard \"j\" of \"jeep.\"

Honestly the fastest way to feel the difference is to say \"ćao\" and \"čamac\" back to back a bunch of times and pay attention to where your tongue physically is. If your tongue is touching the same spot both times, you\'re not doing it right yet.

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[R as a Vowel]]
Words like #strong[prst] \(finger\), #strong[krv] \(blood\), and #strong[Srbija] \(Serbia\) don\'t have a vowel next to the r — the r itself acts as the vowel#degmark . This trips up English speakers constantly because our instinct is to sneak a little \"uh\" sound in there \(like \"puh-rst\" or \"kuh-rv\"\).

Don\'t do that. The trick is to trill\/tap the r and just hold it there a beat, letting it carry the syllable on its own#degmark  — no vowel sneaking in before or after it. Say it slower than feels natural at first, and resist the urge to add anything. If you can say \"Srbija\" without an \"uh\" anywhere near that first r, you\'re doing it right.

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[Consonant Clusters \(hv-, zdr-, šk-...\)]]
English just doesn\'t start words with clusters like these, so your mouth wants to add a vowel to break them up. Don\'t.

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
- #strong[hvala] — start with the \"h\" as a real breathy sound \(like clearing your throat lightly\)#degmark , then go straight into the v, no gap.
- #strong[zdravo] — z and d basically blend into one motion, then roll straight into the r.
- #strong[škola] — š and k run together with zero space between them, then the \"o.\"
]
The trick that helped me most: say the word slow and broken up into its actual letters first \(š...k...o...la\), then gradually squeeze the gaps shut until it\'s one smooth word. Your mouth will eventually stop panicking about clusters that don\'t exist in English.

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[The l\/o Alternation]]
You\'ll notice a lot of words where an \"l\" at the end of a word or syllable turns into an \"o\" sound#degmark  — this shows up a ton in past-tense verb forms \(the -o ending you see everywhere, like radio, igrao, čitao\). That \"o\" used to be an \"l\" and in a lot of dialects\/contexts it still softens toward an \"l\" sound, but standard pronunciation rounds it into \"o.\"

Practically: if you see a verb ending in -o and it feels like it should end in -l based on the rest of the word\'s logic, that\'s exactly what\'s happening#degmark  — don\'t fight it, just say the \"o.\" You don\'t need to overthink the history of it, just recognize the pattern when you see it so it doesn\'t throw you off mid-sentence.

